trix, band width W=9 (offsets -8..+8), row by row over the read length, columns = band window within the reference window. About 150 rows × 17 columns ≈ 2550 cells × 3 operations ≈ 8k ops per read. 431k reads × 8k = 3.4 billion ops — too slow in pure Python (about 30–60+ minutes... actually Python does about 10–50 million simple ops/sec on a tight loop with list accesses — this would take hours).\n\nNeed a faster approach. Options:\n\nA) Myers bit-vector edit distance (pure Python with big ints). For edit distance (no clipping), O(len(ref)) big-int ops. But I need semi-global + clipping. Myers can be adapted for semi-global (free clipping at query ends) — actually, for our purpose: find the best alignment allowing free clipping at read ends. Equivalent to: find a reference substring and an alignment of a read substring (contiguous after removing a prefix/suffix) with small edit distance.\n\nTwo-stage approach: use seed diagonal to get approximate placement. Then verify with cheap mismatch counting allowing no gaps (simulated reads shouldn't have indels... maybe). If mismatch count ≤ threshold on the ungapped placement, accept. Only fall back to DP for reads that fail but have good seeds (rare). Simulated reads: substitutions from sequencing errors + mosaic variants, maybe no indels (depends on the simulator). If the simulator includes indels, ungapped counting would fail. But nonsense SNVs are SNVs. For robustness, do ungapped counting first, then banded DP fallback.\n\nUngapped mismatch counting on the diagonal: comparing read[i] with ref[d+i] over the overlap length. About 150 comparisons per read → 431k × 150 = 65 million comparisons ≈ fast in numpy, or even pure Python (about 20–60 seconds if we use numpy vectorization per read).\n\nBetter way: convert read and reference to numpy byte arrays once. Per read: ref_slice = genome_np[d:d+len], mask Ns, count mismatches with vectorized != → about 2–5 µs overhead + vectorized. Total probably 2–5 minutes. No problem.\n\nFor reads where ungapped mismatches > threshold (e.g., >6% of overlap), try banded DP fallback (with clipping). If that also fails → unmapped. Most reads will pass ungapped.\n\nThen, pileup: for each mapped read with an accepted placement (genomic start d, end d+len, possibly clipped span), record base+quality per genomic position for positions within the read's aligned span. Store pileup counts per position in a dict/Counter: pos → Counter of base (with quality filter, e.g., Q≥20). 431k reads × ~140 positions ≈ 60 million increments — heavy in pure Python, but Counter update on a loop... about 60 million dict ops ≈ 2–4 minutes. Acceptable. Or use numpy cleverly: for each read, vectorized positions = arange, and accumulate per-position counters via per-read dicts. Simpler: iterate.\n\nActually smarter: what we ultimately need is per-position base counts only at positions where alt ≠ ref. Two-pass approach:\n1. First pass: count coverage per position (numpy array of length chr9, 138M int16 → 276 MB... use only CDS±150 positions? Coverage is needed everywhere reads map, but variants are called only within CDS). Count per-position depth into a dict or numpy array restricted to CDS positions (1.44M positions) — map genomic position→index via offset within merged intervals (use searchsorted on interval starts).\n2. For each position in CDS with depth ≥8 (or ≥10), collect base counts from reads overlapping it. Instead of storing all base counts, only re-scan reads in a second pass for candidate positions? That's still heavy.\n\nSimplest: single pass building per-position base counts only for positions in CDS (1.44M). Read overlap with CDS positions: for each read, find CDS positions within its span (via searchsorted on intervals), and for those positions, increment counts[base]. Reads in intronic/flanking regions (within the CDS±150 target but outside CDS) don't contribute to CDS pileup — good, saves work. Most reads overlap CDS (since they're exome reads). Estimated inc