{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"reasoning","text":"Something is very off. With only 20k reads, 120 stop-gain candidates with AF ~0.3, depth ~3658?? \"AF=0.3518 (1287/3658)\" — at the 20k read subset, depth 3658 at a single position is impossible (total mapped bases = 12386 reads × ~140bp ≈ 1.7M; depth 3658 means reads are being piled up at a repetitive locus). This smells like paralog collapse: reads from DOCK8-family pseudogenes/paralogs (DOCK8 has many processed pseudogene segments… in fact DOCK8 is notorious for segmental duplications/pseudogenes on chr9 — DOCK8P1..P13?) all mapping to DOCK8. Similarly ZNG1A. AF ~0.3 across dozens of positions spanning the whole gene = systematic paralogous mismapping, not true variants.\n\nWhy didn't my ambiguity filter catch it? Because with seed voting, the true DOCK8 position wins on votes (the read matches DOCK8 with a few paralogous mismatches; the paralogous copy isn't in my target index — only CDS±100 of protein-coding genes is indexed; pseudogenes aren't indexed → reads get placed on DOCK8 with mismatches; per-read mismatch count ~2–6 is within my ≤max(4, 5%) tolerance).\n\nKey insight: my uniqueness check only compares diagonals within the target index. Paralogous sequences absent from the index can't be detected that way. Need a different strategy:\n\n1. Require near-perfect alignment: reads from the true locus have 0–1 mismatches (aside from variants/errors); reads from a paralog have fixed paralogous differences. If we require ≤2 mismatches per read (depending on length) and count only near-perfect reads, then paralogous reads get excluded and the false AF signal disappears. But then true mosaic variant reads (1 mismatch = the variant) are still kept. Sequencing errors add occasional mismatches. Set threshold: ≤2 mismatches for reads ≥100bp, ≤1 for shorter? But quality-trimmed reads with low-quality tails should have been trimmed by best_window to a high-identity window…\n\nWait, but actually why is the DOCK8/ZNG1A region the only one affected? Both are at chr9 ~164k–420k (DOCK8 region chr9:286k–420k; ZNG1A is chr9:163k–179k?). Hmm, ZNG1A is chr9p? These two gene regions probably have pseudogenes. Also the candidates show AF ~0.25–0.45 — the \"alt allele\" = the paralogous consensus sequence at ~30% of reads (i.e., reads from paralogous copies ≈ 30% of the total coverage at those exons; probably multiple pseudogenes each contributing).\n\nHmm, wait, actually — is DOCK8 the planted gene? The task: \"high-confidence mosaic nonsense SNV within a highly loss-of-function-intolerant protein-coding gene.\" DOCK8: LOEUF ~0.19? Actually DOCK8 is fairly LoF-intolerant (LOEUF ~0.15–0.2, pLI high ~0.99?). Hmm. DOCK8-related disease: autosomal recessive (DOCK8 deficiency), so haploinsufficiency isn't a disease, but its gnomAD LOEUF is still low (~0.2). ZNG1A? Not known as LoF-intolerant.\n\nBut this pattern (dozens of \"variants\" at AF ~0.3 across the entire gene) is clearly mismapping, not planted mosaic variants. The planted variant should be a single position with AF clearly deviating from 0.5 (e.g., 0.1–0.4) and supported by high-quality reads, in a gene without this systematic pattern.\n\nAlso mapped reads: only 12386 out of 20000 mapped (62%). Hmm, that's low. Where did the other 7614 go? Some are reads from UTR/non-coding captured regions (can't map because my target is CDS±100) — expected ~? Exome capture: CDS 1.42M vs full exon ~4.2M → ~66% of exome reads should fall within CDS±100… if the exome target is whole exons, ~55–65% mapping is plausible. But there are also reads from pseudogenes that failed the ambiguity check… and reads with too many mismatches.\n\nHmm, wait — actually, could many unmapped reads be from genes whose CDS I excluded (transcript_type filter)? I only kept transcript_type == \"protein_coding\". Fine.\n\nAlso possibly: reads with indels fail ungapped placement. If the simulator introduced indels… let me check the distribution of mismatches among placed reads, and investigate unmapped reads by attempting DP. First, address the paralog problem with stricter identity and per-position consistency:\n\nBetter approach — mutual consistency (\"self-correction\"): for each position, the reference base supported by the majority of near-perfect reads defines the true base; reads carrying a \"variant\" at AF ~0.3 across hundreds of positions in the same gene, each read carrying multiple such alt alleles simultaneously, is a hallmark of paralogous mapping. True mosaic variants: reads from the mutant cell lineage carry only that one variant (plus random errors). Test: for each candidate alt read, count how many other candidate alt alleles that same read carries. True mosaic alt read: ~0–1 additional. Paralogous read: many.\n\nThis is a powerful discriminant and easy to implement: after pass 1, for each mapped read, we have the set of candidate positions it overlaps; count co-occurrence of alt alleles. But storing per-read candidate co-occurrence in pass 1 requires knowing the candidates (2-pass: pass 1 pileup → candidates; pass 2 re-map and count co-occurrence + evidence). We already have pass 2 for stop_gained candidates; extend to all candidates (3428 positions — checking 3428 positions per read with np.isin… use a dict/set lookup only on overlapping positions: for each read, positions p array; check candidates within [pmin, pmax] via searchsorted on candidate position array → few per read; then check base matches).\n\nThen, final filter: a candidate is \"clean\" if its alt reads carry few other candidate alleles (median ≤1?) and the gene doesn't show a systemic multi-variant signal. Alternatively, more simply: require alt-read specificity: fraction of alt reads carrying 0 additional candidate alleles ≥ 0.8.\n\nAdditional filters:\n- Require near-perfect alignment for counted reads? For the paralog problem: paralogous reads usually have ≥1 paralogous mismatch; at the variant position itself, the alt read = paralogous read, which also carries other mismatches. If we require mismatches ≤ 1–2 (excluding the candidate position itself) for counting… hmm, but for true mosaic variant reads: mismatches = variant (1) + errors (~1–2 at Q20). So requiring total mismatches ≤2 might drop some true variant reads. Better to use the co-occurrence test which directly targets the systematic pattern.\n\n- Also, strand balance and mapping: paralogous reads might map predominantly on one strand? No guarantee.\n\nAlso reconsider: should we include all exons (not just CDS±100) in the index? That wouldn't fix paralogs.\n\nWait — but hold on. Are DOCK8/ZNG1A even captured in the first place? Depth 3658 at a position in a 20k subset is absurd — this means ~3658 reads × base overlap at that position = ~12–25% of all reads pile up there?? 12386 mapped reads, depth 3658 → ~30% of all mapped reads hit position 289540. That can't be paralogous mismapping of a single-copy exome… unless the \"exome\" simulation actually targeted a small panel! Wait: total reads 431k × 140bp = 60M bases; if the target is the whole chr9 exome (~1.4M CDS), average depth ~42×… at 20k subset, average depth ~2. Depth 3658 at one position means the data is enriched for a small set of regions!\n\nHmm! Perhaps the simulator sampled reads from just a small number of genes (amplicon-panel-like) — e.g., ~50–200 genes. Then the huge pileup at DOCK8/ZNG1A = those genes are actually heavily covered targets, and the AF ~0.3 pattern spanning both genes across many positions is… hmm, systematic alt alleles across many positions at AF ~0.3 = still looks like paralogous/homologous mapping, or contamination, or the simulator simulated two haplotypes and these genes are in a region with a homologous pseudogene.\n\nActually wait — the simulation is: two haplotypes (maternal/paternal) with differences, mosaic variant on one… no, haplotype differences are at AF 0.5. AF ~0.3 across many positions… hmm, if the simulator sampled reads from a \"tumor\"-like mixture: 30% of reads from an altered sequence (e.g., paralogous sequence or edited sequence)?\n\nLet me look at the data: what's the actual coverage distribution across genes? Compute per-gene depth in the full run. And check which genes are covered. First, run the full dataset (needed anyway), then investigate.\n\nAlso, unmapped fraction: check why reads don't map. If the panel includes UTRs/introns or non-coding genes, my CDS±100 target will miss them. Could extend the target to all exons ±100 (of all genes? at least pc genes). Let me first run the full data and profile.\n\nAlso potential issue: reads from DOCK8/ZNG1A paralogs mapping to these genes with 5–8 mismatches (≤5% of 150 = 7) — my tolerance is a bit loose. Tighten WINDOW_MM_FRAC to 0.03, and MIN_CLIP_MM=3? Error rate at Q20: ~1%; Q-scores here are mostly 30+? Quality: characters seen are mostly @..F (Q30-37), with some low-quality tails. At 150bp, expected errors ~0.5–1.5. True reads have ≤2 mismatches typically; paralogous reads are usually ≥3 (fixed differences). Tightening to 2–3% should separate them. But low-quality tails produce clusters of errors — clipping handles the ends.\n\nLet me refine: WINDOW_MM_FRAC = 0.02 (max 2% mismatches in the aligned window, floor max(2, ...)), MIN_ALIGNED_LEN=60. Then paralogous reads (≥3 mismatches within 150bp = 2%) — borderline… hmm, 2% of 150 = 3. Some paralogous reads might pass. The co-occurrence test is the robust discriminator. Let me implement both.\n\nActually, one more consideration: maybe those DOCK8/ZNG1A \"variants\" are real — i.e., the simulator spiked in reads from a paralogous sequence to create decoys. The task explicitly says \"high-confidence mosaic nonsense SNV within a highly LoF-intolerant protein-coding gene\" — decoys are expected. DOCK8 LOEUF ≈ 0.19 (gnomAD v2), pLI ~0.99? Hmm… let me recall: DOCK8 is quite constrained: LOEUF around 0.19, yes. ZNG1A: LOEUF ~0.6? Not very constrained. But the systematic AF~0.3 multi-position pattern = decoy (paralogous mapping), regardless of the gene's constraint. The true variant should be a solitary nonsense SNV (single position) in a highly LoF-intolerant gene, with clean supporting reads.\n\nPlan:\n1. Run the full dataset with the current code (collect stats, per-gene coverage).\n2. Add co-occurrence filter + tighter mismatch tolerance.\n3. Re-run, examine stop_gained candidates: true one should be isolated.\n\nAlso improve handling of unmapped reads (62% mapped is too low):\n- Check unmapped reasons: add counters: no_vote (below MIN_VOTE), ambiguous, window_fail.\n- Include all exons (pc genes) ±100 in the index target — recovers reads starting in UTR. But hmm, wait: actually if this is a small panel, coverage is concentrated; whole-exon targets might help capture UTR reads. Let me profile first.\n\nLet me run the full data now (in background with generous yield time; estimated pass1 ~2–4 min, plus index construction)."}}
