{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"reasoning","text":"Processing about 7,200 reads/sec. 431k reads ≈ 60 seconds per pass. While waiting, thinking about co-occurrence (cooccurrence) design.\n\nPass 2 (evidence collection) design — extend it as follows:\n1. Collect read-level evidence for all candidates (not just stop_gained) — 3,428 candidates on a subset; the full run may produce more/less. Per-read check: candidate positions within the read span via np.searchsorted on a sorted candidate position array; for each overlapping candidate position, compare the base at that position in the read with the alt; count the number of \"other candidate alt alleles\" carried by that read.\n2. Per-candidate statistics: fraction of alt reads carrying 0 other candidate alt alleles (\"clean fraction\"), median number of co-occurring alts.\n\nFiltering: high-confidence mosaic variant = clean fraction ≥ 0.8 AND no other candidates with the same AF cluster within the gene? Simpler: just use the clean fraction.\n\nImplementation in run_pass: add an optional `cand_positions` (sorted np array of positions), `cand_alt` (base code), `cand_idx_map`. Per read: after computing p (positions) and b (bases):\n```\nif cand_positions is not None:\n    lo_i = np.searchsorted(cand_positions, p[0])\n    hi_i = np.searchsorted(cand_positions, p[-1], side='right')\n    if hi_i > lo_i:\n        cand_idx_in_read = np.searchsorted(cand_positions, p)  # alignment...\n```\nHmm: for each candidate position cp within [p0, p_last], find the read base at cp: p is contiguous (no gaps since it's ungapped) → index within read of cp = cp - p[0]. Therefore:\n```\ncps = cand_positions[lo_i:hi_i]\nj = cps - p[0]   # index into the read's base array\nbases_at_c = b[j]  (b is filtered by keep! careful: b must be the full base array of the alignment window; quality-filtered positions are marked)\n```\nHowever, b was filtered by keep (Q≥20). Use unfiltered base/quality arrays with a valid mask: bq_full = ql[left:right]; keep_full = (bq_full>=BASE_Q)&(b_full<4). For evidence positions, require keep at j; otherwise, skip (low quality → don't count as carrier).\n\nFor each read, co-occurrence count: for candidate k (the one under evaluation... we need per-candidate aggregation): for each candidate ci overlapping the read where base==alt[ci] and keep → the read carries allele ci. Append this read's carrier set to a per-candidate list? Storage: per candidate, count: alt_reads_total, alt_reads_clean (carrying only this candidate allele among all candidates), sum of other-carried counts.\n\nImplementation: build the read's carrier set S (list of candidate indices carried). For each ci in S: increment carrier_count[ci]; increment clean_count[ci] if len(S)==1; otherwise add len(S)-1 to others_sum[ci].\n\nNote: candidates here should be restricted to positions passing the pileup filter — no problem.\n\nThen selection: for stop_gained candidates, require clean_frac ≥ 0.8 and a decent number of alt reads. Also, exclude candidates where the gene shows massive multi-position signals? The clean_frac criterion captures this: paralog-derived reads carry many alleles → clean_frac ~0.\n\nFurthermore, for the true mosaic variant: alt reads carry exactly 1 allele (the variant itself) + random errors. Random errors rarely reproduce an exact candidate alt at another candidate position (candidates require ≥3 alt reads). So true variant clean_frac ≈ 1.0.\n\nOne caveat: if the sample also has genuine heterozygous germline variants, reads from that chromosome carry that germline variant too... if the germline het variant passes the candidate filter (AF~0.5, alt≥3), then the mosaic variant on the same haplotype would co-occur with it → clean_frac <1. Hmm. In the simulated data, the \"germline\" may be identical to the reference except for the mosaic. But to be safe, make the criterion clean_frac ≥ 0.6 and combine with other QC (strand balance, AF range, base quality), and manually inspect th