{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"reasoning","text":"Now the overall picture is clear:\n\n- Only 4,623 CDS positions have depth ≥10× (0.3% of the CDS). Average CDS depth is 15, but this is dominated by a small number of extremely high-depth positions.\n- The stop-gained candidates are all in DOCK8 (chr9:164k–421k region) and ZNG1A (chr9:163k–179k region), with depths ranging from 61 up to 58,144.\n\nThis is unmistakably a \"decoy\": reads originating from DOCK8/ZNG1A pseudogenes/homologous regions are being erroneously mapped to these genes. The fact that depth varies widely by position (61→58k) suggests that different fragments of the pseudogene sequences are mapping to different DOCK8 exons. Wait, actually the depth pattern along DOCK8 increases from about 61 (exons around 390–420k... hmm, DOCK8 spans chr9:286k–421k in my candidate list, and positions 214998 and 164015–178965 are ZNG1A... wait, ZNG1A is chr9:163k–179k? Hmm, but 214998 was annotated as DOCK8).\n\nHmm wait, let me reconsider. Actually, this pattern might not be paralogous mismapping of exome reads, but rather the data might contain reads derived from a \"contaminating\" sequence, or perhaps the simulator generated reads from the DOCK8/ZNG1A locus with two haplotypes, and the two haplotypes differ at many positions (AF about 0.3 vs 0.5)? AF about 0.3 at hundreds of positions spanning two genes...\n\nAlternative hypothesis: the simulation sampled reads uniformly from the entire chr9 genome (not exome!), i.e. about 0.4× of chr9, but additionally enriched for a specific panel? No wait — only 4,623 CDS positions have depth ≥10, and those are concentrated in DOCK8/ZNG1A. The rest of the exome is essentially depth 0. So this data is a \"targeted panel\" covering DOCK8, ZNG1A, and probably a handful of other genes, not the whole exome.\n\nLet me profile the per-gene depth and see which genes are actually covered. The true target genes (highly LoF-intolerant and carrying mosaic nonsense mutations) must be among the covered genes!\n\nIf only DOCK8/ZNG1A are covered at high depth, then hmm — DOCK8 itself might be the intended gene! DOCK8 is LoF-intolerant (LOEUF ≈ 0.19, pLI ≈ 1.0? Let me recall gnomAD v2.1.1: DOCK8 obs_lof ~0, exp_lof ~45 → LOEUF ≈ 0.1–0.2; pLI is high). But the systematic AF~0.3 pattern across hundreds of positions spanning DOCK8+ZNG1A strongly suggests paralogous mapping: the \"alt allele\" is a pseudogene consensus present at about 30% of reads. Hmm, but wait — why 30%? If reads come equally from DOCK8 and its pseudogene copies... the number of DOCK8 pseudogene copies on chr9 is... hmm, not sure.\n\nWait a sec, another thought: DOCK8 is located in a segmental duplication-rich region, and ZNG1A is nearby too? ZNG1A is chr9p21? Hmm wait: ZNG1A (zinc finger GTPase activating protein 1A) hmm. Positions 163,000–179,000 — that's chr9p24. DOCK8 is chr9p24.1 (chr9:286k–421k on p24.1). Both are on 9p24. Segmental duplications are abundant around chr9p24! In fact, chr9p has large segmental duplications (the 9p24 region includes the DOCK8/ZNG1A region in segmental duplication blocks). Hmm...\n\nWait, actually I recall that ZNG1A/DOCK8 are in a region with known segmental duplications... not sure. Anyway let me check the reference sequence: are there nearly identical copies of the DOCK8/ZNG1A exonic sequences elsewhere on chr9? I can computationally test this: take the sequence of the DOCK8 exon at chr9:289540 and search for exact matches elsewhere on chr9.\n\nAlso — importantly — the true mosaic variant might be in DOCK8/ZNG1A itself, buried among the paralogous noise! But \"high confidence\" means it should be distinguishable: e.g. a position where the alt is supported only by reads with perfect matches elsewhere (true mosaicism), in contrast to paralogous positions where alt reads carry other mismatches.\n\nThe co-occurrence analysis I planned can distinguish these:\n- Paralogs: each alt read carries dozens of other \"candidate alt alleles\" (pseudogene signature).\n- True mosaicism: alt reads carry no other candidate alleles (clean).\n\nAlso the true variant might have AF around 0.3 like the decoys... or it might differ. Let me run the co-occurrence analysis on all 4,626 candidates.\n\nAdditional important check: which genes are covered at all (per-gene depth profile)? Let me compute: for each protein-coding gene, the median depth across its CDS positions. Then I'll know the panel composition. If other genes (e.g. known haploinsufficient genes) are covered at moderate depth, the true variant should be there. If only DOCK8/ZNG1A are covered, the true variant is within them (and decoys = paralogous reads).\n\nWait, hmm, actually reconsidering: the 164,515 unmapped reads (38%)! If the panel includes other genes whose CDS is in my index, reads should map. What's unmapped is: (a) reads from UTR-only or non-coding panel targets, (b) paralogous reads with too many mismatches (excluded by my strict filters — though I place reads with ≤5% mismatch, so a paralogous read with 5–8 mismatches would be either excluded or clipped), (c) reads with indels, (d) low-complexity reads.\n\nAlso note: \"exome reads\" — the README says \"The task's FASTQ contains chr9 reads.\" Not necessarily exome; the prompt says exome reads. Fine.\n\nLet me write an analysis script that re-runs pass 1 but also collects:\n1. Per-gene coverage profile (median/mean depth of CDS positions per gene).\n2. All candidates with depth and AF.\n3. Co-occurrence: for each mapped read, which candidate alt alleles it carries.\n\nTo keep runtime reasonable, modify run_pass in the analysis script to optionally compute co-occurrence in a single pass (candidate positions are known after pass 1 → so a second pass is needed). Pass 1 is 70 s, pass 2 is about the same.\n\nActually, a simpler approach: write a standalone script `scripts/cooccur.py` that imports functions from output/analysis.py, re-runs pass 1 (rebuilding the index, ~15 s), calls candidates,