s chr9q34.11! Epileptic encephalopathy, autosomal dominant. LOEUF ≈ 0.06? Highly constrained.\n- STXBP1? chr9q34.11! Also epileptic encephalopathy, haploinsufficiency, LOEUF ≈ 0.05? pLI 1.0. Highly constrained!\n- EHMT1 chr9q34.12 — Kleefstra syndrome, haploinsufficiency, LOEUF ≈ 0.1?\n- SETDB1 chr1? No.\n- HNRNPK? chr9q21.32 — AU-Kline syndrome, haploinsufficiency, LOEUF ≈ 0.08?\n- TRAF2? chr9q34?\n- NTRK2 chr9q21.33? LOEUF is moderate.\n- CACNA1B chr9q34?\n- SPTAN1 chr9q34! EIEE5, LOEUF ≈ 0.03? Highly constrained.\n- QDPR? chr4.\n- SNAP25? chr20.\n- GRIN1? chr9q34.21! NDD, LOEUF ≈ 0.03? Highly constrained.\n- GABBR2? chr9q22.31.\n- KCNQ1? chr11.\n- …\n\nchr9q34.3 (NOTCH1, TSC1, STXBP1, DNM1, EHMT1, COL5A1, ENG, SPTAN1...) and 9p24 (DOCK8, ZNG1A, VLDLR, RNF38...) are gene-dense regions.\n\nGiven that the data is heavily targeted to the chr9:164k-421k region (DOCK8/ZNG1A locus) — wait, could the whole dataset actually be targeting the 9p24 region, and the decoys are pseudogenes, and the true mosaic variant is in DOCK8? Or in ZNG1A? Is ZNG1A LoF-intolerant? ZNG1A LOEUF ≈ 0.35? I don't remember it being particularly constrained.\n\nDOCK8: is it \"highly\" LoF-intolerant? From gnomAD v2.1.1 constraint: DOCK8 has pLI ≈ 1.0? Let me think: DOCK8 observed LoF variants: 1? Expected about 30-40 → LOEUF ≈ 0.1-0.2, pLI about 1. Hmm, I think DOCK8 pLI is 1.00 and LOEUF ≈ 0.15. Actually recalling DOCK8 LOEUF ≈ 0.15? Reasonably confident it's in the top decile of constrained genes.\n\nBut wait — the co-occurrence analysis might reveal that all DOCK8/ZNG1A candidates are dirty (paralog-derived). In that case, where is the true variant? Maybe a position where clean reads exist. Or maybe the true variant is at moderate depth in another gene — but per the coverage stats, only 4,623 positions have depth ≥10, all probably DOCK8/ZNG1A. So the panel is only targeting the DOCK8/ZNG1A region. Therefore, the true mosaic nonsense variant must be in DOCK8 or ZNG1A (the only covered genes), and the decoys are paralogous reads from pseudogene copies.\n\nHmm, wait, but if the true variant is in DOCK8 — then DOCK8 must be a \"highly LoF-intolerant\" gene (which it is, constraint-wise). And the decoy pattern = reads from DOCK8 pseudogenes (there are multiple DOCK8 pseudogenes; e.g., the DOCK8P1..n family). ZNG1A might also have pseudogenes, or ZNG1A reads might cross-map.\n\nBut wait — why would both DOCK8 and ZNG1A be covered in parallel? Maybe the capture targeted the entire 9p24 region, or maybe ZNG1A coverage comes from reads of ZNG1A pseudogene copies mapping to ZNG1A... ZNG1A depth 3-17k vs DOCK8 up to 58k. Both have high depth.\n\nHmm, actually — alternative hypothesis! The data might not be an exome panel, but rather reads from a region of chr9 containing a ~250kb segmental duplication block at 9p24 ( encompassing DOCK8, ZNG1A, and their paralogous copies elsewhere on chr9). The \"alt\" consensus at AF≈0.3 = the sequence of the paralogous copies. The true mosaic variant is a single clean position.\n\nOr!!! Another thought — maybe the simulator generated reads from two sequences: the real DOCK8/ZNG1A locus, and a \"pseudogene\" sequence (a diverged copy), at a ratio of about 70:30. The task = calling mosaic variants in the presence of pseudogene contamination (like the classic GBA/SMPD1 problem). High-confidence mosaic nonsense variant = a clean position where alt is supported by reads with no other divergence.\n\nLet me wait for the co-occurrence results. If exactly one candidate has clean_frac ≈ 1.0 (and it's nonsense), that's the answer.\n\nAnother consideration: if the paralogous copies are in the index target, my mapper might place true DOCK8 reads incorrectly... no, pseudogene CDS isn't in the index (probably not a protein_coding annotation), so no problem.\n\nBut wait — what about the direction of paralogous mapping: reads from pseudogenes map to DOCK8. True DOCK8 reads also map to DOCK8. At each DOCK8 CDS position, depth = true coverage + pseudogene-derived coverage (when the pseudogene copy contains a sequence homologous to that exon). The depth varying from 61→58k across exons = different pseudogene copies cover different subsets of exons. Position 289540 (depth 58k) = an exon with the highest copy number of homology.\n\nInteresting: AF of the paralogous \"alt\" = pseudogene-derived reads / total ≈ about 0.3.\n\nFor the true mosaic variant at position X: depth_X = true_cov + paralog_cov(X). If X lies within an exon with paralogous homology, the AF gets diluted: AF_obs = mosaic_AF × true_cov / (true_cov + paralog_cov). Hmm.\n\nOK, waiting for results. Meanwhile, let me also think about validation: after identifying the candidate, extract its supporting reads, BLAST-style check: the supporting reads should match DOCK8 perfectly outside the variant. Non-supporting reads at the same position = reference base.\n\nAlso, I should double-check that the true variant isn't being filtered out by the MIN_AF=0.02 / depth thresholds... if the true AF is low (e.g., 0.1), it should still pass.\n\nAnother check: what if the true variant is homozygous/heterozygous (AF about 0.5)? \"Mosaic\" suggests AF below 0.5. Some decoys are AF 0.5 (271630 AF=0.544, 371447 AF=0.504, 311996 0.493). Hmm.\n\nOK, let me wait for the run."}}
